5417] [17592186483349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483349 #edb/ref 1071 "|  | $Y=3$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,3),(2,6)\\})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1430584e-5e6b-5a21-98c2-3433956718bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483349 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,3),(2,6)\\}) & =P((X,Y)=(2,3))+P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(2,3)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.25+0.5 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483350 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483350 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483350 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483350 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=3{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "178b0d68-b124-5a5b-940e-16d447e5211f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483350 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{-5x},$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =3{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x{e}^{-5x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483371 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=6$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c8f03a1-964a-5fd4-a60f-a2aa229186e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483371 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(6) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483414 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483414 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483414 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483414 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d215478d-b523-59e2-bf03-ca21eb9b301e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483414 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3 & =3 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=3$ is the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483433 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483433 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05100288-b9ed-59fd-b7a9-9a2592fbbb50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483433 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483434 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483434 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ba397eb-27d1-5f99-9aee-673204cdeaff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483434 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483438 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483438 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "118abe47-2b28-5f68-9db7-3d02dd65e766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483438 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186445827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483442 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483442 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b35f51c-e940-5ca1-a6eb-b4d04e88b3d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483442 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483443 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b54e950-07e6-508f-8bbf-26f7181e85f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483443 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186266056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483450 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483450 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e0d18ce-acf0-5440-b4c0-fe1c45eb2401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483450 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186259490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483453 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid 